<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Río Salado</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1340">1340</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Tarifa (1340), Battle of Salado · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Río Salado was fought on 30 October 1340 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarifa">Tarifa</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xi-of-castile">Alfonso XI of Castile</a> and Afonso IV of Portugal defeated the Marinid sultan Abu al-Hasan and Yusuf I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, who had crossed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> to besiege Tarifa. It was the last major attempt by a North African power to invade the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a> in 1344.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-rio-salado.jpg" alt="Battle of Río Salado" width="320" height="290" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_batalla_del_Salado_(1340).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After Alfonso XI's victory in the Teba campaign of 1330, Muhammed IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> turned to the Marinid sultan Abu al-Hasan 'Ali, who in 1333 sent a fleet and 5,000 troops that helped Granada take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>. Abu al-Hasan then gathered a great army to reverse the Christian advances of the previous century. His fleet of 60 war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> and 250 other ships under Muhammad ibn Ali al-Azafi destroyed the Castilian fleet at the Battle of Getares on 8 April 1340, killing Admiral Alfonso Jofre de Tenorio. The sultan crossed the straits on 14 August, and with Yusuf I laid siege to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarifa">Tarifa</a> on 22 September.</p><p>Believing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> would need months to rebuild its fleet, Abu al-Hasan laid up most of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a>. Afonso IV of Portugal sent a fleet under Manuel Pessanha and paid for 15 Genoese galleys under Micer Gil Bocanegra, and with new ships from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> the Christians cut the sultan's supply route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> in October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Alfonso XI left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> on 15 October with a relief army, joined the Portuguese king and crossed into enemy territory with about 20,000 men. The sultan lifted the siege and took up a position on a hill between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarifa">Tarifa</a> and the sea, with Yusuf on a neighbouring hill. Contemporary chronicles gave the Muslim armies hundreds of thousands of men, figures the historian Joseph O'Callaghan considered greatly exaggerated. During the night Alfonso slipped 1,000 horse and 4,000 foot into Tarifa, but the sultan was told no Christians had entered the town.</p><p>On the morning of 30 October the Castilians forced the crossing of the Salado and broke into the sultan's camp, while the troops hidden in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarifa">Tarifa</a> struck it at the same time. When Alfonso was nearly drawn into hand-to-hand fighting, Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gil-alvarez-carrillo-de-albornoz">Gil Álvarez Carrillo de Albornoz</a> seized his reins. Attacked from front and rear, the Marinid army fled, and the Granadan contingent broke before the Portuguese. The battle lasted three hours.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The pursuit showed little mercy. Several of the sultan's wives were killed, among them Fatimah, daughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, and his son Abu Umar Tasufin was captured. Abu al-Hasan crossed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> that night. Control of the straits passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and the Genoese. Algeciras fell after a two-year siege in 1344, but an attempt on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> failed; peace came in 1350 after Alfonso XI died in his camp during the Great Plague, and Castile captured Gibraltar only in 1462.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Río Salado” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Alfonso XI won the decisive <b>Battle of Río Salado</b> in 1340 over the Marinids of Morocco and Yusuf I of Granada and took Algeciras in 1344, ending large North African interventions in the peninsula, though he died of plague while besieging Gibraltar in 1350.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The joint defeat of the Marinids and Yusuf I at the <b>Battle of Río Salado</b> in 1340 ended large North African interventions, but Granada exploited Castile's civil wars: Muhammad V took and razed Algeciras in 1369.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-granada">Emirate of Granada · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Then came the reverses: a Portuguese–Castilian coalition crushed Abu al-Hasan's army at the <b>Battle of Río Salado</b> near Tarifa in 1340, and in 1344 Castile took Algeciras, ending the Marinid presence in Iberia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
